    "temperamento-title" => "TEMPERAMENT",
    "temperamento-text" => "Temperament, which we could also call 'character' or 'personality,' arises from the interaction of hereditary characteristics (the genetic heritage) with the living environment: people, places, experiences. Since environments change, temperament also transforms. To some extent, one can also decide how they want to be or become. It cannot be said in the abstract that a character is good or bad; much depends on the contexts in which one finds themselves and the people they meet. A certain behavior, which depends on a particular character, may be useful and appreciated in one situation and considered harmful and annoying in another; one may be liked by some and disliked by others.<br>
	Certainly, to govern a change, we must first have hypotheses about how we are now. This is why we invite you to reflect on these five areas, which are indicative of a certain temperament.
</p>

<h3>Emotional Stability</h3>
<span class='text'>This area refers to the quality of mood, that is, the greater or lesser presence of positive states of mind such as joy or serenity, or, conversely, the prevalence of negative moods, which can manifest in crying, gloominess, or sullenness. The prevalence of positive states of mind predisposes us to greater emotional stability, while negative moods make us more emotionally unstable. This partly depends on us and partly on the contexts, which can always change. Therefore, if you repeat the test after some time, you may notice that the result can be very different.</span>

<h3>Extroversion and Sociability</h3>
<span class='text'>We consider these two areas together because they are closely intertwined and, in some respects, overlapping. There is no positive or negative: in our Western culture, uninhibitedness, openness to others, and sociability are mostly considered positively, while in other cultures, reserve is more appreciated. In general, we can say that variety is a good indicator of a society's health: the shy, cautious, and reserved are as necessary as the friendly, courageous, and sociable. The important thing is to become aware of one's characteristics and own them.</span>

<h3>Conscientiousness</h3>
<span class='text'>Again, as with the previous areas, it is difficult to say what is positive and what is negative. It may seem immediately preferable to deal with reliable, consistent, conscientious people, while it might seem less inviting to interact with unreliable, inconsistent, and duty-lacking individuals. However, it is not uncommon for very orderly people to become very predictable and boring over time, while individuals who are imprecise and disorganized appear in certain situations as original, creative, and pleasantly unpredictable.</span>

<h3>Response to Novelty</h3>
<span class='text'>How does one react to new things, to unexpected stimuli? A very intense response can indicate open-mindedness but also impulsiveness and lack of control, while a very low reactivity can mean prudence and reasonableness but also fear and closed-mindedness. Again, the greater or lesser appropriateness depends on the contexts.</span>",
    "temperamento-results" => "The representation of temperament that emerges from your responses is provisional, neither positive nor negative: it is how you see yourself at this moment. Over time, even within a few months, you might have a different view of yourself.",

    "universo-title" => "MY<br>PERSONAL UNIVERSE",
    "universo-text" => "We are constantly bombarded by countless stimuli. But what is truly important to us? What will fade away quickly, and what will last longer, perhaps for years? It is important to ask ourselves these questions, to take stock of what truly matters, because it not only makes us aware of our present situation but also projects us into what will be our future world.",
    "universo-results" => "You can consult your universe over time, like a diary: which interests remain and which disappear? Are there interests that change orbit, moving from a more internal to a more external one or vice versa? And try asking yourself: why?",
    "area-choose-title" => "Choosing a Path",
    "area-choose-text" => "Interests arise from exposure to experiences: new environments, different people, successes, or failures can create new interests or extinguish previous ones, especially at your age, characterized by rapid changes. However, in general, it is important to question your interests because it gives you an openness to reality and an orientation toward the future.",
    "area-choose-results" => "Keep in mind that interests do not necessarily coincide with abilities. For example, you might be interested in becoming a great skier or a famous dancer but lack the necessary talents to achieve those goals.<br><br>The profile resulting from the questionnaire may be very differentiated across different areas. This means you already have fairly clear ideas about what interests you. Conversely, you might have very similar results, with little differentiation between the various areas. This could mean that you have a wide variety of interests or, on the contrary, that you have not yet structured articulated and solid interests. It might be time to look around more attentively.",
